    id: "greetings",
    title: "Hello, thank you, sorry: Chinese courtesy",
    blurb: "Say hello, thank you, sorry and goodbye, give your name, and know when to use the polite 您.",
    deck: "Greetings and courtesy",
    body: `
<h3>Hello</h3>
<p><b>你好</b> <i>nǐ hǎo</i> is "hello", literally "you good". It is safe with anyone your age, at any hour.</p>
{{ex:你好！|Nǐ hǎo!|Hello!}}
<h3>The polite you: 您</h3>
<p>English has one "you". Chinese has <b>你</b> <i>nǐ</i> for friends, classmates and people your age, and <b>您</b> <i>nín</i> to show respect: to older people, to a teacher, and from staff to a customer. Using <b>您</b> with a friend sounds stiff, never rude.</p>
{{ex:您好！|Nín hǎo!|Hello! (polite)}}
{{ex:王老师，您好！|Wáng lǎoshī, nín hǎo!|Hello, Teacher Wang!}}
<h3>Thank you and sorry</h3>
<div class="tbl"><table><tr><th>You say</th><th>They answer</th></tr><tr><td class="k">谢谢！ (thank you)</td><td class="k">不客气。 (you're welcome)</td></tr><tr><td class="k">对不起。 (sorry)</td><td class="k">没关系。 (that's OK)</td></tr></table></div>
{{ex:谢谢！|Xièxie!|Thank you!}}
{{ex:不客气。|Bú kèqi.|You're welcome.}}
{{ex:对不起。|Duìbuqǐ.|I'm sorry.}}
{{ex:没关系。|Méi guānxi.|That's OK.}}
<p><b>对不起</b> is a real apology, for a mistake you made. For a small inconvenience, like squeezing past someone or asking a favor, the lighter <b>不好意思</b> <i>bù hǎoyìsi</i> is more natural. To start a question to a stranger, say <b>请问</b> <i>qǐngwèn</i>, "may I ask". Among family and close friends, Chinese says thank you less often than English does.</p>
<h3>Names</h3>
<p>To give your name, say <b>我叫</b> <i>wǒ jiào</i> and your name. Chinese names put the family name first: in <b>王明</b>, Wang is the surname. To ask a stranger's surname politely, say <b>您贵姓？</b>, literally "your honorable surname?". Answer with <b>我姓</b> and your surname. <b>贵</b> is only for the other person. Friends often call each other by the full name, like <b>王明</b>; in Chinese that sounds friendly, not cold.</p>
{{ex:请问，您贵姓？|Qǐngwèn, nín guìxìng?|Excuse me, what's your last name?}}
{{ex:我姓王。|Wǒ xìng Wáng.|My last name is Wang.}}
{{ex:我叫王明。|Wǒ jiào Wáng Míng.|My name is Wang Ming.}}
<h3>What to call people</h3>
<p>Titles come after the surname: <b>王老师</b> (Teacher Wang), <b>王先生</b> <i>Wáng xiānsheng</i> (Mr. Wang), <b>李女士</b> <i>Lǐ nǚshì</i> (Ms. Li). Call your teacher <b>老师</b>, a driver <b>师傅</b> <i>shīfu</i>, and a server in a restaurant <b>服务员</b> <i>fúwùyuán</i>.</p>
{{ex:服务员！|Fúwùyuán!|Excuse me! (calling a server)}}
<p>To leave, say <b>再见</b> <i>zàijiàn</i>, "see you again". One more trap: <b>你好吗？</b> <i>Nǐ hǎo ma?</i> is a real question about how someone is doing. For a casual "hi", just say <b>你好</b>.</p>
{{ex:再见！|Zàijiàn!|Goodbye!}}
<div class="tip"><b>Most common mistake:</b> calling a server <b>小姐</b> <i>xiǎojiě</i> (miss). In mainland China it now suggests a sex worker. <b>小姐！</b> → <b>服务员！</b></div>
`,
