    title: "是: A is B",
    blurb: "Say who you are and where you are from with 是, and say what you are not with 不是.",
    deck: "About me",
    body: `
<h3>One verb for am, is and are</h3>
<p>English changes "be" for every person: I am, she is, they are. Chinese has one word, <b>是</b> <i>shì</i>, and it never changes. It joins two nouns: A 是 B.</p>
{{ex:我是美国人。|Wǒ shì Měiguórén.|I'm American.}}
{{ex:她是老师。|Tā shì lǎoshī.|She's a teacher.}}
{{ex:我是学生。|Wǒ shì xuésheng.|I'm a student.}}
<p>There is no word for "a" or "the" either: "a teacher" is just <b>老师</b>. With a name, <b>我是王明</b> <i>wǒ shì Wáng Míng</i> means "I'm Wang Ming": people say it when they introduce themselves or answer the phone, while <b>我叫王明</b> simply tells your name.</p>
<h3>Not: 不是</h3>
<p>Put <b>不</b> before <b>是</b> to say "is not". Remember the tone change from lesson 3: before a fourth tone, 不 rises, so it is <i>bú shì</i>.</p>
{{ex:他不是中国人。|Tā bú shì Zhōngguórén.|He isn't Chinese.}}
<h3>Countries, people and languages</h3>
<p>Add <b>人</b> (person) to a country to name its people: <b>美国</b> (America) gives <b>美国人</b> <i>Měiguórén</i>, <b>中国</b> (China) gives <b>中国人</b> <i>Zhōngguórén</i>. The language is a different word: <b>中文</b> <i>Zhōngwén</i> is Chinese, <b>英语</b> <i>Yīngyǔ</i> is English.</p>
{{ex:王明是中国人。|Wáng Míng shì Zhōngguórén.|Wang Ming is Chinese.}}
<h3>The describer comes first</h3>
<p>As in English, a word that describes a noun goes before it: <b>美国学生</b> is an American student, <b>中文老师</b> a Chinese teacher. Chinese keeps this order even where English does not, so learn it now.</p>
{{ex:他是美国学生。|Tā shì Měiguó xuésheng.|He's an American student.}}
{{ex:李老师是中文老师。|Lǐ lǎoshī shì Zhōngwén lǎoshī.|Teacher Li is a Chinese teacher.}}
<h3>Also: 也</h3>
<p>English puts "too" at the end: "I'm a student too." Chinese puts <b>也</b> <i>yě</i> (also) right before the verb.</p>
{{ex:我也是学生。|Wǒ yě shì xuésheng.|I'm a student too.}}
<h3>Not for adjectives or age</h3>
<p>Both sides of 是 are nouns, so pick the right one: <b>美国</b> is the country and <b>美国人</b> the person. <b>我是美国</b> would mean "I am America".</p>
<p>是 joins a noun to a noun. It does not go before words like tall or busy (the next lesson shows what does). Age takes no 是 either: "I'm 25" is <b>我二十五岁</b> <i>wǒ èrshíwǔ suì</i>, literally "I twenty-five years", and lesson 17 shows how it works.</p>
<div class="tip"><b>Most common mistake:</b> using 是 for every "is", as English uses "be". <b>她是高。</b> (she is tall) → <b>她很高。</b> Keep 是 for noun = noun: <b>她是老师。</b></div>
`,
