    id: "adj",
    title: "Adjectives with 很 (no 是)",
    blurb: "Describe people and things with 很, 不 and 太…了, without the 是 that English makes you want.",
    deck: "Describing things",
    body: `
<h3>Adjectives work like verbs</h3>
<p>English needs "be" before an adjective: she <i>is</i> tall. In Chinese, an adjective is already a full predicate, like a verb, so there is no 是. Instead, a plain statement usually takes <b>很</b> <i>hěn</i>.</p>
{{ex:她很高。|Tā hěn gāo.|She's tall.}}
{{ex:我很忙。|Wǒ hěn máng.|I'm busy.}}
{{ex:我很累。|Wǒ hěn lèi.|I'm tired.}}
<h3>很 is often just glue</h3>
<p><b>很</b> means "very", but in a simple statement it is mostly there to make the sentence sound complete. <b>我很好</b> is just "I'm fine". Without 很, a bare adjective sounds like a comparison: <b>她高，我不高。</b> When you really mean "very", stress 很 or use a stronger word.</p>
{{ex:我很好。|Wǒ hěn hǎo.|I'm fine.}}
{{ex:她高，我不高。|Tā gāo, wǒ bù gāo.|She's tall; I'm not.}}
<h3>Not: 不 + adjective</h3>
<p>To say "not", put <b>不</b> right before the adjective, with no 很 and no 是: <b>不忙</b> <i>bù máng</i>, <b>不贵</b> <i>bú guì</i>. Watch the tone: 不 rises before a fourth tone.</p>
{{ex:我不忙。|Wǒ bù máng.|I'm not busy.}}
{{ex:咖啡不贵。|Kāfēi bú guì.|The coffee isn't expensive.}}
<h3>Too: 太 … 了</h3>
<p>"Too" is <b>太</b> before the adjective and <b>了</b> at the end. It also works for delight: <b>太好了！</b> means "great!".</p>
{{ex:太贵了！|Tài guì le!|That's too expensive!}}
{{ex:太好了！|Tài hǎo le!|Great!}}
<h3>More about 很</h3>
<p>In English, "very" is optional. In Chinese, a plain statement without 很 sounds unfinished, as if you were about to compare two things. In casual talk people also use <b>好</b> in place of 很: <b>我好累</b> <i>wǒ hǎo lèi</i> means "I'm so tired".</p>
<p>Here are adjectives you can use right away: <b>忙</b> busy, <b>累</b> tired, <b>高</b> tall, <b>贵</b> expensive, <b>好</b> good, <b>大</b> <i>dà</i> big, <b>小</b> <i>xiǎo</i> small, <b>冷</b> <i>lěng</i> cold, <b>热</b> <i>rè</i> hot. Each one works the same way: subject, then 很, 不 or 太, then the adjective.</p>
<h3>Before a noun</h3>
<p>An adjective that describes a noun goes before it, as in English: <b>好朋友</b> <i>hǎo péngyou</i>, a good friend. With 很 in front, add 的 to link it: <b>很好的朋友</b>, a very good friend.</p>
<p>Why does <b>他是好朋友</b> have 是? Because 好朋友 is a noun with a describer in front: 是 joins him to "good friend", noun to noun. The rule is about adjectives that stand alone after the subject.</p>
{{ex:他是好朋友。|Tā shì hǎo péngyou.|He's a good friend.}}
{{ex:很好的朋友|hěn hǎo de péngyou|a very good friend}}
<div class="tip"><b>Most common mistake:</b> putting 是 before an adjective, because English says "is". <b>他是高。</b> → <b>他很高。</b> And not both: <b>他是很高。</b> → <b>他很高。</b></div>
`,
