    id: "ma",
    title: "Questions with 吗 and 呢",
    blurb: "Ask yes/no questions with 吗, answer them the Chinese way, and ask back with 你呢.",
    deck: "Questions and connectors",
    body: `
<h3>A statement plus 吗</h3>
<p>English turns words around to ask: "You are a teacher" becomes "Are you a teacher?". Chinese keeps the statement exactly as it is and adds <b>吗</b> <i>ma</i> at the end. That is all.</p>
{{ex:你是老师吗？|Nǐ shì lǎoshī ma?|Are you a teacher?}}
{{ex:你忙吗？|Nǐ máng ma?|Are you busy?}}
{{ex:你也是学生吗？|Nǐ yě shì xuésheng ma?|Are you a student too?}}
<p>Everything else stays in place: 也 still goes before the verb, and an adjective still needs no 是. This works with any statement you already know. <b>你好吗？</b> <i>Nǐ hǎo ma?</i> is built the same way, which is why it is a real question about how someone is doing, not a casual "hi". Adding 吗 never changes the order of the words before it. In writing, the question ends with the full-width question mark ？. One limit: 吗 is only for yes/no questions. A question with a word like "what" or "where" never takes it, as the next lesson shows.</p>
<h3>Let 吗 do the asking</h3>
<p>English speakers raise their voice at the end of a question. In Chinese the tones must stay as they are, so a rising voice can change a word. 吗 already makes it a question; it has the neutral tone, short and light.</p>
<h3>There is no yes or no</h3>
<p>Chinese has no single word for yes or no. You answer with the verb or adjective from the question: yes is <b>是</b> or <b>忙</b>, no is <b>不是</b> or <b>不忙</b>. You can add the full sentence after it.</p>
{{ex:是，我是老师。|Shì, wǒ shì lǎoshī.|Yes, I'm a teacher.}}
{{ex:你是中国人吗？|Nǐ shì Zhōngguórén ma?|Are you Chinese?}}
{{ex:不是，我是美国人。|Bú shì, wǒ shì Měiguórén.|No, I'm American.}}
{{ex:我不忙。|Wǒ bù máng.|No, I'm not busy.}}
<p>The safe answer repeats the verb or adjective of the question. To <b>你忙吗？</b>, say <b>很忙</b> <i>hěn máng</i> or <b>不忙</b>, not 是.</p>
<h3>And you? 你呢？</h3>
<p>To send the same question back, add <b>呢</b> <i>ne</i> to the person: <b>你呢？</b> means "and you?". It saves you repeating the whole question.</p>
{{ex:我很好，你呢？|Wǒ hěn hǎo, nǐ ne?|I'm fine. And you?}}
<div class="tip"><b>Most common mistake:</b> asking with your voice only, as English does. <b>你是老师？</b> with a rise → <b>你是老师吗？</b> And answering 是 to everything: <b>你忙吗？ 是。</b> → <b>你忙吗？ 很忙。</b></div>
`,
